WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
File handling is one of the most common ways a C++ program touches the outside world.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:15.000
A file path may come from a user, a configuration entry, a command line, a service workflow, another process, or an operating system component.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.875
Even when the path is internal, the file contents, metadata, permissions, and filesystem behavior can influence what the program does next.

4
00:00:22.875 --> 00:00:27.375
That makes file handling both a reliability boundary and a security boundary.

5
00:00:27.375 --> 00:00:34.875
Unsafe handling can lead to data loss, accidental disclosure, corrupted state, unreliable processing, or access to files the application never

6
00:00:34.875 --> 00:00:36.000
intended to use.

7
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:45.000
A program may read the wrong file, overwrite useful output, keep temporary data longer than expected, or trust malformed content because it came from

8
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.125
a familiar directory.

9
00:00:46.125 --> 00:00:49.875
The defensive goal is not to fear every file operation.

10
00:00:49.875 --> 00:00:55.125
The goal is to design file operations with clear trust boundaries and predictable behavior.

11
00:00:55.125 --> 00:01:03.750
The code should make it obvious which locations are allowed, which inputs are validated, which operations can create or overwrite data, and how

12
00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:04.875
failures are handled.

13
00:01:04.875 --> 00:01:07.125
Safe file handling is layered engineering.

14
00:01:07.125 --> 00:01:14.250
Path policy, content validation, least privilege, RAII cleanup, temporary file discipline, platform awareness, logging, tests, and review all support

15
00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:15.000
each other.

16
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.375
No single library call proves a design is safe.

17
00:01:18.375 --> 00:01:24.000
A maintainable design makes file assumptions visible, narrow, and testable before they become production incidents.
